    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Corbin Carroll** (81.56) and **James Wood** (76.28) headline our lineup as the premier offensive anchors. **Corbin Carroll** benefits from a high-upside matchup against Painter, where his speed profile and order placement amplify his efficiency score despite a minor penalty for the opposing catcher\u2019s pop time. Meanwhile, **James Wood** remains a locked-in start, leveraging an elite 0.419 xwOBA against Holmes to drive his projection toward the upper tier. We also see high-value production from **Josh Naylor** (65.83) and **Gabriel Moreno** (65.72); both benefit from favorable park factors and SP skill matchups that suppress their opponents' effectiveness, moving them well into the \"Strong\" category for our daily Roto push.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench involved **Austin Riley**, whose score of 49.59 placed him in the marginal category but ultimately left him outside the active roster. While **Austin Riley** offers legitimate power, his lower efficiency projection compared to high-floor options like **Brice Turang**\u2014who gains 8% efficiency from his #2 spot\u2014rendered him a tactical sit to preserve our game caps. Elsewhere, we are exercising caution with **Seiya Suzuki**, whose score of 48.62 is depressed significantly by the elite 2.19 xERA of Misiorowski. While **Seiya Suzuki** is technically a starter, his proximity to the 40-point floor is a point of concern; conversely, **Jose Caballero** (57.22) remains active primarily because his speed-oriented profile and the opponent's slow delivery provide the necessary leverage to keep him in the outfield. \n\nWe are monitoring the risk profile of **Trevor Story**, who despite a respectable 55.19 score, faces a penalty for batting 8th and a tough matchup against Miller. Similarly, while **Zach McKinstry** (41.81) barely cleared our floor, the lack of depth for today\u2019s utility spots makes him a volatile hold. We have successfully avoided the basement, keeping players like **Travis Bazzana** (37.80) benched to maintain our efficiency standards. With **Mickey Moniak** confirmed out of the lineup, our focus remains on maximizing the stolen base potential of **Corbin Carroll**, **Brice Turang**, and **Jose Caballero** to bridge the gap in our 5x5 standing.",
